# Static kernel environment for the Mac mini M4 (t8132) bring-up kernel.
#
# m1n1 chainloads the kernel directly -- U-Boot has no t8132 case at all, so
# there is no loader and no loader.conf. config(5)'s `env` directive is the
# only way to set a tunable, exactly as on the Pi 500+.
#
# This points uart(4) at UART0, the Apple S5L UART inside the SoC. It is the
# same UART Apple's own iBoot drives, which is an unusual luxury for a bring-up:
# the correct output can be captured from a second Mac over USB-C before any of
# our code runs, so the settings below are checkable against known-good firmware
# output rather than guessed at. See the apple-silicon-bringup skill.
#
# SOCDEV_PA + EARLY_PRINTF=s5l already give printf up to cninit(), which drops
# early_putc unconditionally. This is what keeps the console alive past that
# point.
#
# Every tag is load-bearing (sys/dev/uart/uart_subr.c:uart_getenv):
#
#   mm: memory-mapped address. 0x3ad200000, from the device tree the target
#       actually boots with:
#
#         serial0: serial@3ad200000 {
#                 compatible = "apple,s5l-uart";
#                 reg = <0x3 0xad200000 0x0 0x1000>;
#
#       Note the node is `status = "disabled"` in Asahi's t8132.dtsi and m1n1
#       enables it while patching the FDT at runtime, so read what arrives in
#       x0 rather than trusting the .dts on disk.
#
#   dt: the uart class. uart_cpu_getdev() defaults to uart_ns8250_class, so
#       without this an ns8250 driver is pointed at Samsung-derived hardware --
#       garbage on the wire rather than silence, and on this platform those are
#       equally indistinguishable from a dead kernel.
#
#       This tag only works because patches/0031 puts the class in
#       uart_class_set and renames it to a single token. uart_getclass()
#       strncmp()s uc_name against the rest of this string, so the old
#       "s5l class" could never have matched here.
#
#   rs:/rw: register shift and width. uart_getenv() hardcodes rs=0, rw=1 and
#       does NOT inherit uc_rshift/uc_riowidth from the class, so both must be
#       given.
#
#       rs:0 -- and this differs from the Pi, deliberately. exynos_uart.h's
#       SSCOM_* constants are *byte offsets* (ULCON 0x00, UCON 0x04, UTRSTAT
#       0x10, UFSTAT 0x18, UTXH 0x20), not register indices like pl011's, so
#       there is nothing to shift. uc_rshift = 0 in uart_s5l_class agrees.
#
#       rw:4 -- the registers are 32-bit, and the device tree says so:
#       `reg-io-width = <4>`.
#
#   xo: rclk, the UART reference clock in Hz.
#
#       24000000, IDENTIFIED BY WHICH DEVICE IT FEEDS rather than by arithmetic
#       that flatters a guess -- the mistake the Pi 500+ bring-up made and paid
#       for on the kernel's first line. The uart node names its clock:
#
#         clocks = <&clkref>, <&clkref>;
#         clock-names = "uart", "clk_uart_baud0";
#
#       and clkref is a fixed-clock with clock-frequency = <24000000>.
#       Corroborated independently by m1n1, which hardcodes
#       #define UART_CLOCK 24000000 for this SoC family.
#
#       Note it does NOT divide evenly: 24000000 / (16 * 115200) = 13.02. That
#       is mildly reassuring rather than alarming, since an integer divisor is
#       exactly what made the Pi's wrong clock look plausible. m1n1's own
#       divisor -- ((UART_CLOCK / baudrate + 7) / 16) - 1 -- gives UBRDIV = 12,
#       i.e. a divisor of 13 and an effective 115385 baud, 0.16% fast and well
#       inside tolerance. The S5L also has a fractional divisor (UFRACVAL) for
#       the remainder.
#
# UNVERIFIED ON HARDWARE. Every value above is sourced, and none has been
# through a boot.
hw.uart.console="mm:0x3ad200000,br:115200,dt:s5l,rs:0,rw:4,xo:24000000"
